      ":question/id": "d70606cc-3373-5e20-bd92-6ee87d5c95c9",
      ":question/problem": "In medieval times, there were $2630$ people living in a city struck by a plague. If the disease resulted in a daily *decrease* in the population at a continuously compounded rate of $13.9\\%$, what was the city's population after $1$ weeks? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.",
      ":question/requires-calculator": true,
      ":question/worked-solution": "The continuously compounded growth equation is $P=P_0e^{rt}$. The initial amount is $P_0=2630$. Since the amount decreases, the signed decimal rate is $r=-13.9/100=-0.139$. The rate is daily, so convert the duration to days: $t=1\\times 7=7$ days.\n\nSubstitute these values and round only the final amount:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= P_0e^{rt} \\\\\n&= 2630e^{(-0.139)(7)} \\\\\n&= 2630e^{-0.973} \\\\\n&= 2630(0.3779474\\ldots) \\\\\n&\\approx 994\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter $1$ weeks, the population was approximately $994$ people."
    }
  ],
  "canonical_example": {
    ":db/id": 17592186059597,
    ":question/id": "90177be7-ab0c-531b-b07b-778004ad6f2b",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5185",
    ":question/problem": "An asset has been depreciating in value for the past $10$ years at an annual, continuously-compounded rate of $6\\%$. If that asset was initially worth $\\$13\\,500$, what is it worth now? Round your answer to the nearest dollar.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "The continuously compounded growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0e^{rt}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have an initial amount of\n\n$$\nP_0=13\\,500\n$$\n\na rate of $r=-0.06$, and a time duration of $t=10$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} P &= P_0e^{rt} \\\\ &= 13\\,500e^{(-0.06)(10)} \\\\[3pt] &= 13\\,500e^{-0.6} \\\\[3pt] &= 13\\,500(0.548\\,81...) \\\\[3pt] &\\approx 7\\,409. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $10$ years, the value of the asset has decreased to $\\$7\\,409$."
  },
